messages:
maria: FYI found a dup-charge bug in the onboarding sample app when the client retries on 429s. Repro steps in the browser via fast reload + service worker.

maria: Steps: click buy -> service worker intercepts, does fetch to /v1/generate -> server returns 429 briefly -> client retries immediately and ends up sending twice. We saw two charges in stripe. :facepalm:

devon: ouch. Is the SDK adding an idempotency key for browser calls today?

sanjay: Not for in-browser short-lived requests. We only auto-add Idempotency-Key on server SDKs right now. Browser SDK lets the app set header manually.

maria: Right. The sample app was naive and retried on any transient error. Also the service worker queued the retry without deduping the in-flight request.

alex: Could we implement a client-side replay-guard in the JS SDK that uses a short-term in-memory request fingerprint + jittered backoff to avoid duplicate side-effects?

devon: Proposal: SDK auto-set Idempotency-Key when a request contains mutable operations (payments, webhooks) unless app passes allowUnsafeReplay=true. Also make the retry policy default to exponential backoff with full jitter and respect Retry-After header.

maria: Agree on respecting Retry-After. Browser clocks are flaky though. Maybe keep a small grace window.

sanjay: Sketch of approach: 1) generate idempotency key: `${clientId}:${sha1(payload)}` 2) attach header `Idempotency-Key: <uuid>` 3) keep a short LRU of keys (30s) to prevent duplicates from service worker retries 4) default backoff = base 200ms * 2^n, jitter = uniform(0, base)

alex: Code sample for attaching header in SDK:```js// inside fetch wrapper
const key = opts.idempotencyKey || generateIdempKey(body);
headers['Idempotency-Key'] = key;
const res = await fetch(url, {method: 'POST', headers, body});
if (res.status === 429) {
  const ra = res.headers.get('Retry-After');
  const wait = ra ? parseInt(ra,10)*1000 : calcBackoffWithJitter(attempt);
  await sleep(wait);
  // retry logic
}
```

sanjay: also add opt-in stateful SW integration: expose sdk.peekInFlight(key) so SW can dedupe with clients that share the same origin.

devon: One risk: hashing body for idemp key may map different side-effecting params to same key. Better to let apps opt in which fields are fingerprinted, or default to random UUID per logical operation.

maria: For sample app quick fix, I pushed a patch to only retry non-idempotent operations on network errors, not on any 429. That reduced dupes in manual tests.

ci-bot: build #4821 started for branch fix/sampleapp-retry-filter (tests: unit+e2e)

alex: We should document the semantics clearly in onboarding: what SDKs auto-idempotize, what headers we set, and provide a tiny SW snippet showing dedupe by Idempotency-Key.

devon: Agree. I'll draft a doc page and a lightweight PR for the JS SDK to: auto-idempotency for payments, backoff w/ full jitter, and optional SW hooks.

maria: Thanks — can we also add a debug flag so the SDK logs when it attaches an idempotency key? Help on support calls.

sanjay: Yup — `REDWOOD_SDK_DEDUPE_LOG=true` style. Will wire it into the next minor.
